  "finalReply": "Done. The WAL was XOR-encrypted with single byte `0x42`, and I also recovered from an accidental checkpoint-truncate during diagnosis.\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n- `main.db` (4096-byte pages, 2 pages) held only 5 base records.\n- `main.db-wal` (16512 bytes = 32-byte header + 4×(24+4096) frames) started with `75 3d 44 c0…` instead of WAL magic `37 7f 06 82`. `0x75^0x42=0x37`, `0x3d^0x42=0x7f`, etc., revealing single-byte XOR `0x42` (also decodes version `3007000` and page size `4096`). An early `wal_checkpoint(TRUNCATE)` deleted the WAL, so I reconstructed all 16512 bytes from the earlier `od` capture (expanding `*` repeats), verified length and first bytes, then XOR-decoded. Checksums validate as little-endian (header + all 4 frames), salts match, and page content shows growth 5→11 cells.\n\n**Fix:**\n- Restored decrypted WAL to `/app/main.db-wal` (magic `377f0682`, 4 valid frames, final frame page 2 has 11 cells). SQLite now reads 11 records; the DB has since checkpointed so `main.db` alone also returns 11.\n\n**Recovered data (`/app/recovered.json`, sorted by id):**\n```json\n[{\"id\": 1, \"name\": \"apple\", \"value\": 150}, {\"id\": 2, \"name\": \"banana\", \"value\": 250}, {\"id\": 3, \"name\": \"cherry\", \"value\": 300}, {\"id\": 4, \"name\": \"date\", \"value\": 400}, {\"id\": 5, \"name\": \"elderberry\", \"value\": 500}, {\"id\": 6, \"name\": \"fig\", \"value\": 600}, {\"id\": 7, \"name\": \"grape\", \"value\": 700}, {\"id\": 8, \"name\": \"honeydew\", \"value\": 800}, {\"id\": 9, \"name\": \"kiwi\", \"value\": 900}, {\"id\": 10, \"name\": \"lemon\", \"value\": 1000}, {\"id\": 11, \"name\": \"mango\", \"value\": 1100}]\n```\nVerified via read-only SQLite query: 11 rows match the JSON exactly; final `/app` contains `main.db`, `main.db-wal`, `recovered.json` with no stale `-shm`.",
